<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Chen Han</h1><p class="st-meta">1360 CE – 1364 CE · capital Wuchang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Yangtze river empire of Chen Youliang, whose great fleet was destroyed by Zhu Yuanzhang at Lake Poyang in 1363, one of the largest naval battles in history.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-youliang">Chen Youliang</a>, the son of a fisherman, rose as an officer in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban</a> regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-shouhui">Xu Shouhui</a> on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. In 1360 he murdered Xu and proclaimed himself emperor of Han, ruling first from Jiangzhou and later from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang">Wuchang</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chen Han controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguang">Huguang</a> and was at first the strongest of the rebel states against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>. Its power rested on a large river fleet of towering warships. Chen ruled by force rather than by building institutions, and the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-shouhui">Xu Shouhui</a> led commanders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-yuzhen">Ming Yuzhen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> to break away.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>An attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> failed in 1360. In 1363 Chen besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanchang">Nanchang</a> and then met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-poyang">Lake Poyang</a>; after weeks of fighting, including fire-ships, Chen was killed by an arrow. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-li-chen-han">Chen Li</a> surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang">Wuchang</a> in 1364, and his lands became part of the future <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chen-han.jpg" alt="Chen Han" width="320" height="380"><figcaption>Image: Joko2468 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Turban_regional_powers_1350%E2%80%931360.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1360 CE – 1364 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wuchang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chen Youliang, Chen Li</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Han (Chen Youliang)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1362&amp;focus=chen-han" aria-label="Chen Han on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7659 1068 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chen Han lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7979 1324L7867 1005M7979 1324L7643 1242"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7867 1005h0M7643 1242h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7979 1324h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7979 1324h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wuchang. <a href="/atlas?year=1362&amp;focus=chen-han">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
